WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
One example, one line.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:13.485
The format most tools accept is chat JSONL: one JSON object per line, each with a messages list of turns that have a role (system, user, or assistant) and content.

3
00:00:13.765 --> 00:00:20.421
OpenAI’s fine-tuning API uses it, and Hugging Face TRL calls it the conversational format.

4
00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:28.573
TRL also accepts a prompt-completion variant, where the turns before the answer go under prompt and the answer under completion.

5
00:00:28.853 --> 00:00:39.861
For the extraction task, an example looks like the block below: the system prompt you will use in production, the document as the user turn, and the gold label as the assistant turn.

6
00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:46.029
A few rules make the difference between a dataset that trains well and one that doesn’t: Match production.

7
00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:51.643
Use the same system prompt and the same user-message layout the deployed model will see.

8
00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:57.021
A model trained on one layout and served with another is being tested off-distribution.

9
00:00:57.301 --> 00:00:59.733
Make the target exactly what you want back.

10
00:01:00.013 --> 00:01:08.909
For extraction, that is the JSON object and nothing else: no “Here are the items:” preamble, unless you want the model to say it every time.

11
00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:10.555
Include the negatives.

12
00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:14.931
Documents with no action items need an empty list as their answer.

13
00:01:15.211 --> 00:01:19.925
Leave them out and the model learns that every document has items, and invents some.

14
00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:22.040
One canonical serialization.

15
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.869
Write labels with sorted keys so identical labels produce identical text; you’ll see why in a moment.
